# TODO - Ternary machine lowering

## Objective

Lower typed C IR into a compact ternary virtual-machine representation suited to
Malbolge instead of translating C operations directly instruction by
instruction.

## Acceptance

- Every IR operation lowers to explicit ternary/runtime operations with defined
  pre/postconditions and no direct host implementation of guest computation.
- Guest-runtime semantic identities supplied by `guest-runtime-and-allocator`,
  including fundamental byte I/O and allocation/runtime helpers, lower to
  executable ternary/Malbolge-oriented operations without host callback
  substitution.
- The stage has deterministic input/output form, rejects malformed or
  unsupported input explicitly, and preserves source/profile provenance needed
  downstream.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- All declared prerequisites are completed: `typed-compiler-ir`,
  `guest-runtime-and-allocator`, `malbolge-specific-optimization-mathematics`,
  and `documentation-readiness-and-implementation-gate`.
- The governing contract now pins the already-authoritative inbound identities:
  `malbolge-typed-ir-v1`, `malbolge-c32-v1`, `malbolge-guest-runtime-v1`,
  `malbolge_guest_runtime_bind_heap`, `malbolge_guest_intrinsic_input_word`, and
  `malbolge_guest_intrinsic_output_byte`, with host fallback explicitly
  forbidden. No ternary-stage wire identity is reserved before implementation.
- `src/compiler/ternary-lowering/` now provides a governed inbound semantic
  projection, deterministic pre-layout target model, and first fail-closed
  lowering application. The implemented slice accepts one-block no-argument
  `i32` constant-return functions, converts exact 32-bit patterns to fixed
  21-trit least-significant-first scalars, and preserves source/module
  provenance without importing typed-IR implementation types.
- `tests/ternary_lowering.rs` adapts the tracked canonical typed-IR golden only
  after complete admission, proves deterministic `return 7` lowering and exact
  source-span/identity preservation, round-trips representative extreme 32-bit
  patterns through the ternary scalar, rejects malformed/identity-drifted
  projections, and rejects a richer admitted typed-IR shape.
- The same lowering boundary now admits typed-IR successful `u8` `ByteInput` and
  `ByteOutput` effects. A valid byte-I/O fixture is admitted, canonicalized,
  restored exactly, projected, and lowered in source order; fabricated
  projections with non-`u8` input, undefined output, duplicate SSA identity, or
  byte-valued return fail closed. These target effects deliberately do not claim
  raw profile-word/EOF or declaration-only intrinsic realization.
- Public `getchar`/`putchar` planning now composes the verified helper and raw
  intrinsic semantics in the exact checked-in guest stdio order, with explicit
  decoded-`i32`/EOF versus emitted-byte return behavior. Tests bind the plan to
  `c-libc-v1.json` and `stdio.c` while leaving both routines
  `contracted_unavailable` until executable target realization is complete.
- The wrapper raw-I/O step now revalidates and realizes through the canonical
  target-profile projection, proving `getchar` uses selected-profile input and
  `putchar` selected-profile output while preserving helper/order/return state.
  Forged plans and colliding profile opcodes fail closed.
- Wrapper-control-flow realization now re-derives the machine plan before
  publishing explicit public stdio execution steps. `getchar` initializes EOF,
  performs machine input, runs helper control flow, guards `VALID`, and returns
  EOF on helper failure; `putchar` runs its low-byte helper, machine output, and
  widened return in exact order. Forged helper execution or profile drift fails
  closed.
- A pure runtime-helper boundary now admits only
  `malbolge_guest_decode_input_word` and `malbolge_guest_output_byte` under the
  current profile. It emits declarative input byte/EOF/status and output
  low-byte
  recipes sourced from runtime/profile authority without host evaluation; tests
  reject unknown helpers, profile drift, and EOF overlap with `0..255`. Direct
  external helper calls are not representable by typed IR yet.
- Runtime-helper realization now expands input decoding into the exact ordered
  null-pointer/byte/EOF/invalid-word exits with explicit result and status
  publication, including header-bound status values `1/0/0/4`. Output
  realization
  retains the exact low-byte mask, and realized stdio wrapper plans carry the
  helper execution plan beside profile-bound machine I/O.
- A separate inbound runtime-intrinsic request now admits only the exact
  `malbolge_guest_intrinsic_input_word` and
  `malbolge_guest_intrinsic_output_byte` identities under `malbolge-2026`,
  lowering them to distinct raw `InputWord`/`OutputByte` pre-layout operations.
  Tests cross-check both identities against the guest-runtime JSON authority and
  declaration header and reject unknown identities or profile drift.
- A target-profile I/O projection now copies the canonical current descriptor's
  exact profile identity, input/output instruction bytes, and EOF word. Runtime
  I/O realization derives machine operations from those fields rather than
  compiler-local `/`/`<` literals; tests compare directly with
  `current_profile()` and reject colliding opcodes or profile drift.
- An instruction-decoder inbound port now supports target-cell encoding without
  copying XLAT1. The encoder requires exactly one graphical source cell for the
  realized opcode at an already-chosen code position; tests exhaust all 94 VM
  decode phases for both current-profile I/O operations and reject missing or
  ambiguous inverse decoders.
- The typed semantic slice now also lowers `i32` bitwise AND and validated
  `i32`-to-`u8` truncation. A canonical round-trip fixture proves the exact
  `value & 255` then truncate path can feed `ByteOutput`, matching the operation
  vocabulary required by `malbolge_guest_output_byte`; fabricated type or SSA
  drift fails closed. Direct runtime-helper call recognition remains separate.
- A layout-resolved startup request now carries the exact ABI/profile/bind
  symbol,
  guest arena pointer/capacity, and user entry identity. Startup planning
  validates
  ABI pointer alignment/address containment plus runtime heap geometry and emits
  exactly one `BindHeap` action before `EnterUserCode`; tests cross-check the
  runtime header/JSON authority and reject identity or extent drift.
- Remaining work includes the rest of the typed-IR operation vocabulary,
  global heap placement and executable bind-call realization, global target
  layout/source sequencing, typed-IR external-call binding, wrapper
  branch-address/source-cell placement, later runtime helpers, target
  serialization, and
  executable Malbolge-oriented lowering evidence. No
  ternary-stage wire identity exists yet.
- Expected durable artifact surface: `compiler/`, `src/`, `tests/compiler/`.
- Required evidence: golden/round-trip or normalized stage fixtures,
  deterministic hashes where promised, and end-to-end lowering regression cases.
- Runtime realization evidence: consume guest-runtime semantic identities
  unchanged and prove their executable ternary/Malbolge lowering, including the
  one-time heap startup bind and declaration-only byte-I/O intrinsic symbols.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `typed-compiler-ir`,
  `guest-runtime-and-allocator`, `malbolge-specific-optimization-mathematics`.
